      "html": "<p>Males become sexually active at a length of about 7 cm. The maximum length is given as about 12 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l129-var-hypancistrus-debilittera-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hypancistrus debilittera occurs in the Rio Bita, a tributary of the Rio Meta in Colombia (Orinoco drainage).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l129-var-hypancistrus-debilittera-2/",
      "html": "<p>The small, nicely marked Hypancistrus debilittera from the Rio Bita has enjoyed great popularity for years.</p><p>A shipment of \"Hypancistrus sp.\" from Colombia arrived without concrete locality information. The animals were 6–8 cm long. The import contained specimens that could easily be assigned to H. debilittera, but also completely spotted animals, and all conceivable transitions between the two. These fish were therefore listed as L129-variant.</p><p>Had all animals been spotted, a new L-number would have been expected. There is still much to explore about these catfishes, which only became known to science through aquaristics, and wild-caught fish are repeatedly needed to judge the natural variation. It remains an open question whether the Hypancistrus of the Orinoco basin hybridize with each other as readily as their relatives in the Rio Xingu have been shown to do.</p>"
    }
  ],
